                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the graveyard: why pilots die`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Over five years, <strong>14 clinically promising pilots</strong> were embedded on single wards and then quietly died — 11 were still “live” at six months and dead by 24. The board no longer asks “does it work in a pilot?” but “will it still be running, and saving, in two years?” Before you design any fix, diagnose <em>why good pilots fail to sustain</em>: knowledge-translation gaps (the evidence never reaches routine practice), structural gaps (no owner, no funding line), and behavioural gaps (the workaround is easier). Your sepsis pilot has 38% consistent users, 44% inconsistent, and <strong>18% on a paper workaround</strong>. Name the top sustainability risk before you spend a pound.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the knowledge-translation tactics`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Allocate the <strong>£1.8M rollout envelope</strong> across a costed menu. Passive dissemination — a trust-wide email, an intranet guideline, a launch webinar — <em>reliably fails</em> and is exactly what killed 11 of your 14 dead pilots. Active, social and structural tactics change practice: <strong>clinical champions</strong> and audit-and-feedback anchor it socially, <strong>EPR forcing-functions</strong> embed it in the record, and education reaches the 44% inconsistent and 18% workaround users. Choose a coherent bundle, decide how hard to lean on the EPR forcing-function versus persuasion, and justify what you deliberately did NOT fund.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Make the board case for the IT investment`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Justify <strong>£1.8M capital + £420K/year</strong> against a <strong>£12M deficit</strong>. Clinical benefit doesn’t fund itself: translate the 18% mortality cut and 1.4-day length-of-stay reduction into the board’s language — <em>freed bed-days, avoided litigation, and CQC/reputational value</em>. The finance director will ask what the case is worth at <strong>40% adoption</strong>, not 75%. Choose the funding route under the deficit, size the regulatory value, and stress-test the case to a credible adoption floor — a case that only works at 75% is not a fundable case.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Routinize under resistance & the 9-month checkpoint`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `It is the simulated <strong>9-month checkpoint</strong> and adoption sits near the 60% gate — clear it or central funding is withdrawn and the pathway reverts to opt-in, the exact pattern that killed 14 tools. But go-live is not the finish line: convert early adoption into <strong>embedded routine</strong> — named ownership, standing audit, EPR default, induction — that survives champion turnover. Then a senior consultant <em>publicly disputes the algorithm’s false-positive rate</em> and threatens to opt his department out. Resistance carries real information. Address the algorithm concern AND keep the structural lever, and name who owns the pathway after the project team disbands.`,

                    r1_rootcause_label:     `Cluster the 14 dead pilots — which root cause killed most of them?`,
                    r1_risk_label:          `Name the sepsis pathway's single biggest sustainability risk.`,
                    r1_target_label:        `Define what "sustained at 24 months" must concretely mean.`,
                    r1_diag_label:          `How rigorously do you diagnose the embedding problem before designing the fix? (%)`,
                    r1_diag_readout:        `Diagnostic rigour before solutioning:`,
                    root_behavioural:       `Behavioural & structural gap — every dead pilot had a guideline, but no owner and no funding line, so the easier workaround always won`,
                    root_evidence:          `Weak evidence — the tools simply did not work well enough once off the pilot ward`,
                    root_technology:        `Technology — the IT was clunky and the integrations broke`,
                    root_funding:           `Pure funding — they all died the moment the pilot grant ended, nothing else mattered`,
                    risk_workaround:        `The 18% on the paper workaround plus the 44% inconsistent users decay back to old habits once the spotlight moves`,
                    risk_mortality:         `That the 18% mortality benefit fails to replicate trust-wide`,
                    risk_cost:              `That the £1.8M is simply never approved against the deficit`,
                    risk_none:              `There is no real risk — a pilot this strong will sustain itself`,
                    target_adoption_owned:  `≥75% consistent adoption AND a named clinical owner with a standing audit — embedded routine, not a live pilot`,
                    target_live:            `Still switched on and technically "live" in the EPR at 24 months`,
                    target_golive:          `A successful go-live and a good 9-month adoption number`,

