                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_behavioural:   `<strong>Right diagnosis.</strong> Every one of the 14 had a guideline; what they lacked was an owner, a funding line, and a workflow where the new way was easier than the workaround. That behavioural-structural gap — not weak evidence — is what your sepsis pathway has to beat.`,
                    coach_r1_evidence:      `<strong>Careful — this isn’t an evidence problem.</strong> Your pilot already cut mortality 18%; so did most of the 14 that died. If you diagnose “weak evidence” you’ll spend the rollout proving efficacy again instead of fixing the owner, the funding line and the workaround. Re-look at the post-mortems.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp framing.</strong> You named the behavioural-structural root cause AND the real risk — the 18% on the workaround and the 44% inconsistent users decaying once the spotlight moves — and defined success as embedded, owned routine at 24 months, not a “live” switch. That is the diagnosis the whole embedding plan now has to neutralise.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>“A pilot this strong will sustain itself” is what the teams behind all 14 graveyard tools believed.</strong> They were all live at six months. Nothing sustains itself in a 7,400-staff trust without an owner, a routine and an audit. Name the real risk.`,
                    coach_r1_live:          `<strong>“Still switched on at 24 months” is not the test.</strong> Eleven of the dead pilots were technically live for a year while clinicians quietly drifted back to the workaround. Define success as consistent <em>use</em> and named ownership, or you’ll declare a victory the data won’t support.`,
                    coach_r1_golive:        `<strong>A good go-live and a 9-month number is exactly the trap.</strong> All 14 had those. The board judges success at 24 months. Define what “sustained” concretely means — adoption AND ownership — or you’re measuring the wrong finish line.`,
                    coach_r1_lowdiag:       `<strong>You’re reaching for tactics before you’ve diagnosed the failure mode.</strong> Skipping the diagnosis is how the last 14 teams jumped straight to a launch event. Spend the rigour here: which gap actually threatens the sepsis pathway?`,
                    coach_r2_passive:       `<strong>Your plan leans on a trust-wide email, a guideline and a webinar.</strong> That is exactly the passive dissemination that killed 11 of your 14 dead pilots — every one had a guideline and none of them stuck. Which of your tactics actually reaches the 18% on the paper workaround? Shift the money to champions, audit-and-feedback and the EPR.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A coherent active bundle.</strong> Embedding it as the EPR default with clinical override, anchored by champions and audit-and-feedback, is what reaches the inconsistent and workaround users. Now make sure the champions are funded to outlast the pilot.`,
                    coach_r2_mandatory:     `<strong>Hard-wiring mandatory, non-overridable alerts buys adoption on paper and breeds alert fatigue in practice.</strong> Clinicians reflexively override, the loudest consultant recruits colleagues to opt out, and a forcing-function without credibility becomes the resistance story. Keep the EPR default — but keep the override and the clinical voice.`,
                    coach_r2_persuasion:    `<strong>Persuasion alone leaves the workaround intact.</strong> Champions and education win hearts, but without the EPR forcing-function the easier old path is still right there — and the 18% on paper will stay on paper. Structural and social tactics together, not one without the other.`,
                    coach_r3_costavoid:     `<strong>That’s the language the board can fund.</strong> Freed bed-days, avoided litigation and the mortality benefit, costed against £1.8M + £420K, turns a clinical story into an investable one. Now show me what it’s worth if adoption lands at 40%, not 75%.`,
                    coach_r3_clinical:      `<strong>“It saves lives and cuts length-of-stay” is true and it doesn’t fund itself.</strong> Facing a £12M deficit, I need freed bed-days and avoided litigation in pounds, and the adoption rate the benefit assumes. Clinical-benefit-only is Common Error number two — translate it.`,
                    coach_r3_mandate:       `<strong>“A safety mandate you can’t refuse” with vague numbers invites exactly the refusal you fear.</strong> A board under a £12M deficit funds costed cost-avoidance, not moral pressure. Give me the bed-days and the litigation figure.`,
                    coach_r3_central:       `<strong>Demanding the full £1.8M of central capital up front, deficit or not, is the ask most likely to be deferred.</strong> An invest-to-save route funded from the bed-days it frees, staged against adoption, is far easier for a board in deficit to say yes to.`,
                    coach_r3_defer:         `<strong>Defer and you’ve chosen the graveyard.</strong> Wait for next year’s budget and the champion moves on, the pilot energy dissipates, and the pathway joins the 14. The deficit will not ease on its own — fund it as invest-to-save now.`,
                    coach_r3_skipvalue:     `<strong>The board cares about more than cash.</strong> A CQC downgrade or a sepsis-mortality headline carries real, sizeable value — leaving the regulatory and reputational case unsized weakens the very argument that wins a deficit board.`,
                    coach_r3_sensitivity:   `<strong>That’s a fundable case.</strong> A cost-avoidance argument that still holds at a realistic adoption floor — not just at the 75% best case — is what survives the finance director’s 40% question. You’ve priced the risk, not just the upside.`,
                    coach_r3_overoptimistic:`<strong>A case stress-tested only above 80% adoption isn’t stress-tested at all.</strong> The finance director’s whole point is what happens at 40%. Set a credible floor and show the case still holds there, or the board will assume the benefit is fiction.`,
                    coach_r4_governance:    `<strong>That ownership survives the hero leaving.</strong> A named clinical owner plus a sepsis governance group, standing audit, EPR default and induction means the pathway doesn’t depend on any one person’s energy. This is the mechanism the 14 dead pilots never had.`,
                    coach_r4_champion:      `<strong>Your whole plan rests on Dr. Ahmed.</strong> In 11 of your 14 failures the tool died within a year of the champion leaving or burning out. What happens to adoption the day Dr. Ahmed takes a sabbatical — and what owns the pathway when no single hero does? Build governance, not dependency.`,
                    coach_r4_project:      `<strong>“Extend the project team and work out handover later” is how handover never happens.</strong> The funding spotlight moves, the team disbands, and ownership falls into the gap that killed the last 14. Name a permanent clinical owner now.`,
                    coach_r4_path75:        `<strong>That’s the difference between go-live and sustainability.</strong> Clearing 60% at nine months means nothing on its own — 11 dead pilots cleared it too. Showing the concrete mechanisms that keep the curve climbing to 75% by 24 months is what convinces a board that has been burned 14 times.`,
                    coach_r4_celebrate:     `<strong>You hit the gate and the team celebrated.</strong> So did the teams behind 11 dead pilots — all “live” at nine months, all gone by 24. Easing off the moment you clear the gate is precisely how the curve decays once the spotlight moves. The real test is still 15 months away.`,
                    coach_r4_overrule:      `<strong>Hard-wiring the alerts harder and framing the consultant as obstruction breeds the opt-out you’re trying to prevent.</strong> His “resistance” contains real information — false positives, alert fatigue, workload. A forcing-function without clinical credibility recruits the whole department against you. Treat the resistance as data.`,
                    coach_r4_concede:       `<strong>Dropping the forcing-function to keep the peace hands the workaround back its advantage.</strong> Opt-out is the exact mechanism that killed 14 pilots. You can address the false-positive concern without surrendering the structural lever — tune the algorithm, don’t abandon the default.`,
                    coach_r4_master:        `<strong>That is how innovation sticks.</strong> Named governance ownership, a credible path to 75% past the 9-month gate, and engaging the consultant’s challenge as design data while keeping the EPR default — you’ve converted a champion-dependent pilot into a clinician-owned routine that survives both the champion leaving and the false-positive fight.`,

