                    insight_embedded:       `Embedded & sustained: you treated sustainability as a distinct competency from piloting. An active, EPR-embedded translation bundle drove adoption past 75%, a costed invest-to-save case won the deficit board, and named governance ownership plus an engaged consultant means the pathway survives the champion leaving. This one does not join the graveyard.`,
                    insight_graveyard:      `Joined the graveyard: adoption never cleared the gate or the plan repeated the exact pattern that killed 14 pilots — passive dissemination, champion dependency, or a forcing-function without credibility. It may be “live” for a while, but the curve will decay once the spotlight moves.`,
                    insight_fragile:        `Live but fragile: you cleared the immediate gate but the three decisions don’t yet hold together. The adoption, the funding case and the ownership tell slightly different stories — enough for the 24-month review to find the seam.`,
                    insight_passive:        `Your bundle leaned on passive dissemination — the email, the guideline, the launch event. That is the single most documented cause of pilot death; every pound there reached almost none of the workaround users.`,
                    insight_champion:       `The plan rests on a single champion. In 11 of 14 failures the tool died within a year of the hero leaving. Without governance ownership, Dr. Ahmed’s sabbatical is the pathway’s expiry date.`,
                    insight_victory:        `You declared victory at go-live or the 9-month gate. Short-term adoption is not sustainability — the board judges this at 24 months, and easing off after the gate is how the curve decays.`,
                    insight_alertfatigue:   `You ran a forcing-function without clinical credibility. Mandatory alerts plus a dismissed false-positive concern breed alert fatigue and reflexive overrides — the structural lever turned into the resistance story.`,
                    insight_unfunded:       `Your investment case stayed below the £1.8M + £420K ask. A clinical-benefit-only or vaguely-mandated case doesn’t fund itself against a £12M deficit — the board needs costed cost-avoidance with adoption sensitivity.`,
                    insight_engaged:        `Adoption is high and clinicians are engaged rather than resisting — you converted the inconsistent and workaround users by treating their resistance as design input, not defiance. That is what makes the routine durable.`,

                    hint_passive:           `Your spend is too heavy on passive dissemination — emails and guidelines reach almost no one on the workaround. Shift the envelope to champions, audit-and-feedback and the EPR forcing-function.`,
                    hint_alertfatigue:      `You have a forcing-function without credibility — mandatory alerts and an unaddressed false-positive concern. Tune the algorithm and engage the consultant, or alert fatigue will hand the department its opt-out.`,
                    hint_champion:          `The pathway depends on one champion. Build a named governance owner, standing audit and EPR default so it survives the day Dr. Ahmed leaves.`,
                    hint_gate:              `Adoption is below the 60% gate — at the 9-month checkpoint that withdraws central funding and reverts the pathway to opt-in. Lean harder on active, EPR-embedded tactics that reach the workaround users.`,
                    hint_case:              `Your investment case is thin against a £12M deficit. Translate the mortality and length-of-stay benefit into freed bed-days and avoided litigation, route it invest-to-save, and stress-test it at 40% adoption.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — adoption past target, sustainability high, clinicians engaged. Lock in named governance ownership and the path to 75% so it survives the champion leaving and the 24-month review.`,

